// _tests_/bit/benchgate/baselineslinux.bit — the two Linux baseline tables for
// the benchmark regression gate, split out of `baselines.bit` when #3934's
// second alloc case took that file over the 800-line limit (E0200). A pure
// move: `baselinesLinuxX64` and `baselinesLinuxArm64` are here VERBATIM,
// every measurement and calibration comment intact, and `baselinesFor` still
// calls both from `baselines.bit` — sibling files in one directory module
// share a scope, so nothing is imported and no call site changed.
// Measured on real x86_64-linux hardware (hl-master, i7-6700@3.4GHz, 6
// vCPUs) by #2932 via `X64GATE_HOST=hl-master STEP=test-benchgate
// scripts/x64gate.sh`, taking the minimum of two independent cold-build runs
// ~10 minutes apart (#2933) — the same "minimum cannot be inflated by noise"
// reasoning the file header gives for `reps`:
//   run 1: collatz 328cs/233472B, mandelbrot 569cs/233472B, fib 101cs/229376B, alloc 1144cs/8818688B
//   run 2: collatz 327cs/229376B, mandelbrot 559cs/229376B, fib 101cs/225280B, alloc 1153cs/8749056B
// The ratio to the macOS/arm64 table above is NOT constant across cases
// (3.5x on fib, 3.9x collatz, 4.6x mandelbrot, 5.0x alloc) — a pure
// host-speed difference would be roughly constant, so part of the gap is
// x86_64 codegen quality, not clock speed alone (filed separately, not
// chased here: #2926 landed the x64 backend's first back-edge safepoint
// guard the same day these numbers were measured). RSS on this host is
// LOWER than macOS/arm64 (Linux's /proc VmHWM accounting differs from
// macOS's `/usr/bin/time -l`), well under its own ceiling.
// objects (#3130) is -1 ("not measured on this platform") on every entry in
// this table: no Linux host reachable this session, same constraint #2932
// already recorded for the CPU/RSS numbers above. The band check in
// benchgate.bit's runCase skips a -1 baseline; the BIT_GC_STATS line is
// still required unconditionally regardless. Object counts should in
// principle be host-architecture-independent (they are a property of the
// source program's control flow and the runtime's allocator policy, not of
// clock speed or codegen) -- but "should in principle" is exactly the kind
// of unverified claim this repo's own rules say not to record as a number;
// leave -1 until someone measures it on real Linux hardware.
// #3615: baselinesMacosArm64() above was bumped +2 per case for #2579's
// sysmon monitor thread, a fixed boot-path allocation. This table is left
// untouched -- not just because every entry here is already -1
// ("not measured"), but because #2579's sysmon thread is DARWIN-ONLY
// (`sched/darwin/`), so even once this platform is measured for real, its
// objects baselines will NOT carry this +2.
fn lxCaseCollatz(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "collatz",
    expected: "524",
    cpuCentis: 327,
    rssBytes: 229376,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: -1,
  }
}

// #3173: this row's `expected` and `cpuCentis` were UNVALIDATED -- both
// carried over from the macOS/arm64 row above without ever compiling and
// running on this platform. `expected: "204866964"` happened to be
// right, but only by luck of timing: until #3168 landed (451c9993,
// "partition the spill pool by register class -- fixes an all-platform
// parallel-move miscompile shipped in 0.1.18"), x86_64 genuinely printed
// a different, wrong answer for this case (726141000, i.e. 3.54x the
// correct summed-iteration count) -- so this row could never have PASSed
// on its own hardware before that fix landed. `cpuCentis: 559` was never
// a measurement of this binary either: it was close to the arm64 row's
// 121cs by the family's ~4.6x ratio (coincidence, not derivation) and
// nowhere near the ~5519cs the miscompiled path actually cost (that
// number is recorded in #3173's own ticket comments, not here, because
// recording a baseline for a miscompiled binary would have ratcheted in
// the wrong figure).
//
// RE-VERIFIED on hl-master (i7-6700, real x86_64 Linux) after #3168,
// 2026-08-17. Host checked idle before and throughout: load average
// 2.6-4.4 on 6 vCPUs (own containers' own load, not contention), %CPU
// idle 85-92% via vmstat, top non-benchmark process ~1% CPU
// (tailscaled), single ssh login. `expected: "204866964"` is now
// CONFIRMED correct -- not carried over, observed: 8 direct runs of the
// exact harness `./make test-benchgate` runs (`BIT_STDLIB=... BIT_BIN=...
// BIT_LIBBITRT=... BIT_BENCH_DIR=... bit-out/bin/bit run
// _tests_/bit/benchgate`), invoked directly rather than through
// `scripts/x64gate.sh` end-to-end because, at the time of this
// measurement, #3227 -- a bug where the batch driver's watchdog
// subshell used bash-only `exec -a`, which dash (this image's /bin/sh)
// rejects -- killed every gate within ~1s of starting regardless of
// the actual work. #3227 (`tools/build/gates.bit`, merged a8e0b012)
// and its duplicate #3231 (`_tests_/bit/importsrun/importsrun.bit`, merged
// b105f8a3) have since landed, so `x64gate.sh` end-to-end works on a
// dash host again -- confirmed 2026-08-17 (#3235) by rerunning
// `X64GATE_HOST=hl-master STEP=test-benchgate bash scripts/x64gate.sh`
// end-to-end: `X64LINUX_EXIT=0`, `benchgate: PASS`. All 8 direct runs
// printed the byte-identical correct value and PASSed (same assertion
// code `./make test-benchgate` runs). cpuCentis
// readings across those 8 runs: 267 278 279 283 284 285 291 291 --
// minimum 267cs, a 24cs (9%) spread -- replacing 559 below, the same
// "minimum cannot be inflated by noise" convention this file's other
// baselines use.
fn lxCaseMandelbrot(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "mandelbrot",
    expected: "204866964",
    cpuCentis: 267,
    rssBytes: 229376,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: -1,
  }
}

// #2982's fib fix does NOT transfer here -- deliberately left at the
// blanket 150 (1.5x). The defect on macOS/arm64 is an ABSOLUTE-headroom
// problem: baseline 29cs, 1.5x ceiling 43cs, only 14cs of absolute
// headroom for scheduling noise that is itself roughly fixed in
// centiseconds, not proportional to the baseline -- that 14cs is what
// real contention filled. hl-master's fib baseline is 101cs (3.5x
// larger), so 1.5x already gives 151cs, i.e. 51cs of absolute headroom
// -- 3.5x the margin that broke on macOS/arm64. Raising this to 190
// would buy nothing against a mechanism this baseline is not exposed to,
// while costing real detection power: it would move the ceiling from
// 151cs to 191cs against a 2x regression at 202cs, shrinking the margin
// from 51cs to 11cs -- reintroducing the thin-boundary problem #2982
// fixed on macOS/arm64, on the one platform with no direct load
// measurement to justify it. If a false positive is ever observed on
// hl-master, that needs its own ticket with fresh measurements from that
// hardware, not a number inferred from this box.
fn lxCaseFib(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "fib",
    expected: "102334155",
    cpuCentis: 101,
    rssBytes: 225280,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: -1,
  }
}

// #3934 changed what this case allocates on EVERY platform, so
// `expected` moves here too: 59995000000 -> 69990000000, each node now
// summing its batch id as well. The value is platform-independent (it is
// the program's own arithmetic, and the closed form gives it
// independently), so recording it here is a fact, not a guess.
//
// cpuCentis/rssBytes below are NOT re-measured for that shape -- no
// Linux host in this session -- and are left at the #2932/#2933 numbers,
// which were taken on the BOXED program before #3862 packed it. That is
// the shape this case has again, so they are the right order rather than
// stale-by-a-factor, but they are inherited and this line is here so the
// next reader does not mistake them for a measurement of the current
// program. #3991 owns re-measuring them on hl-master.
fn lxCaseAlloc(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "alloc",
    expected: "69990000000",
    cpuCentis: 1144,
    rssBytes: 8749056,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: -1,
  }
}

// bench/cases/allocflat (#3934) is deliberately ABSENT from this table:
// never measured on this platform, and omitted rather than guessed --
// the same precedent #2978's churn case set five lines below.
// `baselinesFor`'s per-platform length is what makes that safe.
// #2978's churn case has NOT been measured on this platform (no Linux
// host in this session) -- omitted rather than guessed, matching
// baselinesLinuxArm64's precedent below. baselinesFor's per-platform
// table length means this is safe: main()'s completion check compares
// against len(table), not a hardcoded case count.
fn baselinesLinuxX64(): []Baseline {
  return [
    lxCaseCollatz(), lxCaseMandelbrot(), lxCaseFib(), lxCaseAlloc(),
  ]
}

// linux/aarch64 (scripts/arm64gate.sh) has never been measured on a quiet
// host — #2932 correctly skipped it because this Mac had three concurrent
// builds running at the time (#2933). Returning an empty table here, rather
// than a guess or a borrowed platform's numbers, is what makes
// `baselinesFor` below fail loudly instead of comparing the wrong pair.
// #3615: no entries here to re-baseline, and none will need the +2 sysmon
// bump this ticket applied to baselinesMacosArm64() -- #2579's sysmon
// thread is darwin-only.
fn baselinesLinuxArm64(): []Baseline {
  return []
}
